WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.391
A microservice system has more communication
paths and administrative surfaces than one

00:00:05.391 --> 00:00:10.906
isolated process. Begin by identifying which
routes clients actually need. An API gateway

00:00:10.906 --> 00:00:16.236
can expose those routes, apply common checks and
direct requests to internal services.

00:00:16.236 --> 00:00:21.335
Restricting direct access reduces opportunities
to bypass those checks, but the gateway is

00:00:21.335 --> 00:00:24.904
one control rather than a reason to trust
everything behind it.

00:00:24.904 --> 00:00:29.915
Client application credentials and user identity
answer different questions. A secret

00:00:29.915 --> 00:00:35.043
embedded in a distributed browser or mobile
application cannot be assumed confidential.

00:00:35.043 --> 00:00:40.290
Apply request limits and protect the
infrastructure control plane, while planning
gateway

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.058
capacity and failure behavior.

00:00:42.058 --> 00:00:47.165
Include outbound dependencies in the trust
model: an internal service may call an external

00:00:47.165 --> 00:00:51.818
endpoint that supplies malicious data or
receives sensitive information. Draw both

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:56.924
directions of traffic before deciding where
checks belong. If only the gateway is publicly

00:00:56.924 --> 00:01:00.442
reachable, can an internal billing service skip
authorization?

00:01:00.558 --> 00:01:05.849
No. A compromised internal caller or a mistaken
route can still reach it. Enforce resource

00:01:05.849 --> 00:01:09.494
permissions at a trustworthy boundary that
cannot be bypassed.

00:01:09.558 --> 00:01:14.367
Encryption is useful only when the connection
reaches the intended peer. Certificate

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:19.119
verification links a presented key to a trusted
identity under the client’s policy.

00:01:19.119 --> 00:01:23.927
Accepting every certificate removes that
assurance. A self-signed certificate can be

00:01:23.927 --> 00:01:28.965
deliberately trusted through an authenticated
provisioning process; simply accepting any

00:01:28.965 --> 00:01:32.400
self-signed certificate does not establish the
correct peer.

00:01:32.558 --> 00:01:38.066
Verification includes the intended service
identity, not merely a recognizable issuer.

00:01:38.066 --> 00:01:43.458
TLS protects data while it crosses the
established channel. In mutual TLS, both sides

00:01:43.458 --> 00:01:48.787
authenticate with certificates; this identifies
a workload or client credential, not

00:01:48.787 --> 00:01:53.101
automatically a human user or permission to
perform every operation.

00:01:53.101 --> 00:01:58.441
Protect private keys and plan issuance, renewal
and compromise response. Pinning narrows

00:01:58.441 --> 00:02:03.904
accepted credentials but adds rotation and
recovery responsibilities. Keep authentication,

00:02:03.904 --> 00:02:09.244
confidentiality and application authorization
distinct in the architecture diagram. If a

00:02:09.244 --> 00:02:14.464
client encrypts traffic but accepts any
certificate, has it authenticated the intended

00:02:14.464 --> 00:02:14.889
server?

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:20.519
No. It may establish an encrypted channel to an
impostor; identity verification is

00:02:20.519 --> 00:02:21.180
essential.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:26.584
An internal network should not imply
unrestricted communication. List the connections
each

00:02:26.584 --> 00:02:31.868
workload requires, then restrict both incoming
and outgoing paths. Kubernetes namespaces

00:02:31.868 --> 00:02:37.152
organize resources but do not automatically
isolate their network traffic. NetworkPolicy

00:02:37.152 --> 00:02:40.935
requires enforcement by the selected networking
implementation.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:46.466
Separate intended policy from demonstrated
behavior, and test whether an unrelated service

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:48.404
can reach a protected dependency.

00:02:48.404 --> 00:02:54.015
Outbound restrictions help reduce unwanted
destinations and some SSRF consequences, but
they

00:02:54.015 --> 00:02:59.138
complement rather than replace application
validation. Runtime monitoring can report

00:02:59.138 --> 00:03:04.749
suspicious behavior; an alert is not necessarily
a preventive control. Resource quotas bound

00:03:04.749 --> 00:03:09.994
aggregate namespace allocations, while workload
requests and limits address individual

00:03:09.994 --> 00:03:11.092
resource behavior.

00:03:11.092 --> 00:03:15.720
These measures can reduce blast radius and
resource contention without promising to

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.684
eliminate denial of service. Define who
investigates alerts and how a service is
isolated

00:03:20.684 --> 00:03:25.647
without losing essential evidence. Does placing
billing and search in separate namespaces

00:03:25.647 --> 00:03:27.710
block search from contacting billing?

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:33.483
No. Namespaces alone do not impose that network
boundary; enforced policies and the relevant

00:03:33.483 --> 00:03:35.241
selectors must establish it.
